{"type":"user/message","data":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<system-reminder>\\nA skill is a reusable set of task-specific instructions. The following skills are available in this session:\\n\\n<available_skills>\\n- \`arx-archive-agent-notes\`: Use when adding, auditing, pruning, archiving, restoring, or reviewing Agent Notes in arx-harness; checks every new note for superseded active records, classifies implemented notes by future decision value, deletes rejected notes that no longer prevent a tempting fallacy, and applies the frozen archived/{kind} triplet and manifest rules.\\n- \`arx-code-review\`: Use when reviewing a pull request in the arx-harness repo — orients the reviewer to this codebase's standards (AGENTS.md conventions, defensive patterns, ADRs, quality gates) and the review-specific checks that code alone can't show\\n- \`arx-doc-site-sync\`: Use when publishing, updating, moving, or removing arx Harness documentation website pages; editing website/docs.ts mappings or navigation; diagnosing a page missing from the VitePress site; fixing projected documentation links; or running the docs:dev, docs:check, and doc-sync workflow after website-content changes.\\n- \`arx-doc-standards\`: Use when writing, moving, reviewing, or auditing documentation in the arx-harness repo — choosing hierarchy and detail, separating tutorials from references, checking tutorial progression, trimming doc slop, responding to a verify-doc-budgets failure, or requests like \\"improve the docs\\", \\"audit the docs\\", \\"where should this be documented\\", or \\"this doc is too long\\".\\n- \`arx-find-simplifications\`: Use when working in the arx-harness repo to find non-obvious simplification candidates, write proposed Agent Notes or inline TODO/FIXME/XXX notes, audit or coalesce superseded Agent Notes, or fold worthwhile simplification ideas from another PR; especially for dead, duplicated, speculative, over-built, added-then-removed, or hand-rolled-where-a-dependency-exists surfaces.\\n- \`arx-merging-stacked-prs\`: Use when landing a stack of dependent GitHub PRs (A ← B ← C, where each bases on the one below) onto master, merging a PR whose base is another open PR's branch, or whenever a request mentions \\"stacked PRs\\", \\"PR stack\\", \\"dependent PRs\\", or merging several related PRs in sequence. Requires every same-repository dependency chain to use GitHub's official stacked-PR feature before landing so GitHub owns stack-wide rules, CI, ordering, retargeting, and merge state.\\n- \`arx-pre-push-checks\`: Use before pushing, force-pushing, marking ready for review, or claiming checks pass on a arx-harness branch, and immediately after gh stack sync publishes rewritten branches, to select the smallest tests and checks that cover the outgoing or just-published diff without reflexively running the full repository suite.\\n- \`arx-prose-standard\`: Use when writing, reviewing, restoring, trimming, or auditing prose in the arx-harness repo, including deciding where documentation or comments are required across Markdown, JSDoc, code and test comments, prompts, descriptions, diagnostics, and CLI or UI strings.\\n- \`arx-trim-cot-leakage\`: Use when auditing or fixing prose that reads like a leaked reasoning transcript — dead design-session citations such as (decision N), audit item codes, or §N of uncommitted drafts; change narration such as \\"used to\\", \\"no longer\\", \\"this cut\\"; stack or review vantage (\\"a later PR in this stack\\", \\"rejected in review\\"); reviewer-addressed justifications; control-flow narration; or hedged planning residue in comments, JSDoc, docs, or Agent Notes.\\n- \`graphify\`: Navigate arx Harness architecture and source integrations using the local Graphify AST graph, queries, and reports; regenerate it after source changes without a Claude plugin or inference API.\\n- \`ponytail\`: Forces the laziest solution that actually works, simplest, shortest, most minimal. Channels a senior dev who has seen everything: question whether the task needs to exist at all (YAGNI), reach for the standard library before custom code, native platform features before dependencies, one line before fifty. Supports intensity levels: lite, full (default), ultra. Use on ANY coding task: writing, adding, refactoring, fixing, reviewing, or designing code, and choosing libraries or dependencies. Al...\\n- \`ponytail-audit\`: Whole-repo audit for over-engineering. Like ponytail-review, but scans the entire codebase instead of a diff: a ranked list of what to delete, simplify, or replace with stdlib/native equivalents. Use when the user says \\"audit this codebase\\", \\"audit for over-engineering\\", \\"what can I delete from this repo\\", \\"find bloat\\", \\"ponytail-audit\\", or \\"/ponytail-audit\\". One-shot report, does not apply fixes.\\n- \`ponytail-debt\`: Harvest every \`ponytail:\` comment in the codebase into a debt ledger, so the deliberate shortcuts and deferrals ponytail leaves behind get tracked instead of rotting into \\"later means never\\". Use when the user says \\"ponytail debt\\", \\"/ponytail-debt\\", \\"what did ponytail defer\\", \\"list the shortcuts\\", \\"ponytail ledger\\", or \\"what did we mark to do later\\". One-shot report, changes nothing.\\n- \`ponytail-gain\`: Explain published Ponytail benchmark results and their limits without inventing savings for this repository or model.\\n- \`ponytail-help\`: Explain the six model-independent Ponytail skills installed in arx, their invocation, and the absence of native Claude modes or hooks.\\n- \`ponytail-review\`: Code review focused exclusively on over-engineering. Finds what to delete: reinvented standard library, unneeded dependencies, speculative abstractions, dead flexibility. One line per finding: location, what to cut, what replaces it. Use when the user says \\"review for over-engineering\\", \\"what can we delete\\", \\"is this over-engineered\\", \\"simplify review\\", or invokes /ponytail-review. Complements correctness-focused review, this one only hunts complexity.\\n- \`record-browser-gif\`: Record browser or Web UI interaction demos as optimized GIFs using the available built-in browser, state-based frame capture, and deterministic encoding, then publish to a dedicated assets branch when the task includes attaching the GIF to a pull request. Use when asked to make, record, or generate a GIF that demonstrates a browser workflow, and for every pull request that changes product-user-visible GUI behavior, which MUST include a GIF recorded from the pull request's real server and mode...\\n</available_skills>\\n\\nIf the user names a skill, or the task clearly matches a skill's description, call the \`skill\` tool with the exact skill name before taking task actions. Load all applicable skills, then follow their full instructions. This catalog contains summaries only; do not infer or follow a skill's instructions until it has been loaded.\\nA user may also invoke a skill directly; its <skill_content> block then appears in this conversation. Follow it, and do not call the \`skill\` tool again for that skill.\\n</system-reminder>"}],"source":{"kind":"skill-catalog","form":"catalog","entries":[{"name":"arx-archive-agent-notes","description":"Use when adding, auditing, pruning, archiving, restoring, or reviewing Agent Notes in arx-harness; checks every new note for superseded active records, classifies implemented notes by future decision value, deletes rejected notes that no longer prevent a tempting fallacy, and applies the frozen archived/{kind} triplet and manifest rules."},{"name":"arx-code-review","description":"Use when reviewing a pull request in the arx-harness repo — orients the reviewer to this codebase's standards (AGENTS.md conventions, defensive patterns, ADRs, quality gates) and the review-specific checks that code alone can't show"},{"name":"arx-doc-site-sync","description":"Use when publishing, updating, moving, or removing arx Harness documentation website pages; editing website/docs.ts mappings or navigation; diagnosing a page missing from the VitePress site; fixing projected documentation links; or running the docs:dev, docs:check, and doc-sync workflow after website-content changes."},{"name":"arx-doc-standards","description":"Use when writing, moving, reviewing, or auditing documentation in the arx-harness repo — choosing hierarchy and detail, separating tutorials from references, checking tutorial progression, trimming doc slop, responding to a verify-doc-budgets failure, or requests like \\"improve the docs\\", \\"audit the docs\\", \\"where should this be documented\\", or \\"this doc is too long\\"."},{"name":"arx-find-simplifications","description":"Use when working in the arx-harness repo to find non-obvious simplification candidates, write proposed Agent Notes or inline TODO/FIXME/XXX notes, audit or coalesce superseded Agent Notes, or fold worthwhile simplification ideas from another PR; especially for dead, duplicated, speculative, over-built, added-then-removed, or hand-rolled-where-a-dependency-exists surfaces."},{"name":"arx-merging-stacked-prs","description":"Use when landing a stack of dependent GitHub PRs (A ← B ← C, where each bases on the one below) onto master, merging a PR whose base is another open PR's branch, or whenever a request mentions \\"stacked PRs\\", \\"PR stack\\", \\"dependent PRs\\", or merging several related PRs in sequence. Requires every same-repository dependency chain to use GitHub's official stacked-PR feature before landing so GitHub owns stack-wide rules, CI, ordering, retargeting, and merge state."},{"name":"arx-pre-push-checks","description":"Use before pushing, force-pushing, marking ready for review, or claiming checks pass on a arx-harness branch, and immediately after gh stack sync publishes rewritten branches, to select the smallest tests and checks that cover the outgoing or just-published diff without reflexively running the full repository suite."},{"name":"arx-prose-standard","description":"Use when writing, reviewing, restoring, trimming, or auditing prose in the arx-harness repo, including deciding where documentation or comments are required across Markdown, JSDoc, code and test comments, prompts, descriptions, diagnostics, and CLI or UI strings."},{"name":"arx-trim-cot-leakage","description":"Use when auditing or fixing prose that reads like a leaked reasoning transcript — dead design-session citations such as (decision N), audit item codes, or §N of uncommitted drafts; change narration such as \\"used to\\", \\"no longer\\", \\"this cut\\"; stack or review vantage (\\"a later PR in this stack\\", \\"rejected in review\\"); reviewer-addressed justifications; control-flow narration; or hedged planning residue in comments, JSDoc, docs, or Agent Notes."},{"name":"graphify","description":"Navigate arx Harness architecture and source integrations using the local Graphify AST graph, queries, and reports; regenerate it after source changes without a Claude plugin or inference API."},{"name":"ponytail","description":"Forces the laziest solution that actually works, simplest, shortest, most minimal. Channels a senior dev who has seen everything: question whether the task needs to exist at all (YAGNI), reach for the standard library before custom code, native platform features before dependencies, one line before fifty. Supports intensity levels: lite, full (default), ultra. Use on ANY coding task: writing, adding, refactoring, fixing, reviewing, or designing code, and choosing libraries or dependencies. Al..."},{"name":"ponytail-audit","description":"Whole-repo audit for over-engineering. Like ponytail-review, but scans the entire codebase instead of a diff: a ranked list of what to delete, simplify, or replace with stdlib/native equivalents. Use when the user says \\"audit this codebase\\", \\"audit for over-engineering\\", \\"what can I delete from this repo\\", \\"find bloat\\", \\"ponytail-audit\\", or \\"/ponytail-audit\\". One-shot report, does not apply fixes."},{"name":"ponytail-debt","description":"Harvest every \`ponytail:\` comment in the codebase into a debt ledger, so the deliberate shortcuts and deferrals ponytail leaves behind get tracked instead of rotting into \\"later means never\\". Use when the user says \\"ponytail debt\\", \\"/ponytail-debt\\", \\"what did ponytail defer\\", \\"list the shortcuts\\", \\"ponytail ledger\\", or \\"what did we mark to do later\\". One-shot report, changes nothing."},{"name":"ponytail-gain","description":"Explain published Ponytail benchmark results and their limits without inventing savings for this repository or model."},{"name":"ponytail-help","description":"Explain the six model-independent Ponytail skills installed in arx, their invocation, and the absence of native Claude modes or hooks."},{"name":"ponytail-review","description":"Code review focused exclusively on over-engineering. Finds what to delete: reinvented standard library, unneeded dependencies, speculative abstractions, dead flexibility. One line per finding: location, what to cut, what replaces it. Use when the user says \\"review for over-engineering\\", \\"what can we delete\\", \\"is this over-engineered\\", \\"simplify review\\", or invokes /ponytail-review. Complements correctness-focused review, this one only hunts complexity."},{"name":"record-browser-gif","description":"Record browser or Web UI interaction demos as optimized GIFs using the available built-in browser, state-based frame capture, and deterministic encoding, then publish to a dedicated assets branch when the task includes attaching the GIF to a pull request. Use when asked to make, record, or generate a GIF that demonstrates a browser workflow, and for every pull request that changes product-user-visible GUI behavior, which MUST include a GIF recorded from the pull request's real server and mode..."}]},"role":"user","id":"{{sessionId}}"},"surfaceOp":"append"}
{"type":"user/message","data":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\\"ponytail\\">\\n<skill_resources>\\nBase directory for this skill: {{skillRoot}}/ponytail\\nResolve relative paths mentioned by this skill against the base directory before using them. Load referenced resources only as needed.\\n</skill_resources>\\n\\n<skill_instructions>\\n## arx Harness integration\\n\\nThese are model-independent instructions, not a Claude Code plugin. Use the arx \`skill\` tool or explicit \`/ponytail\` invocation; do not run marketplace installers, hooks, auto-updaters, or write mode flags. Availability requires the session to expose the \`skill\` tool. Follow the repository instructions, requested scope, approval policy, security requirements, documentation obligations, and existing test framework. Never remove required validation or tests to reduce line count. Guidance remains ordinary logged context until compaction; no automatic activation, durable intensity setting, or child-context propagation is installed. For other capabilities, keep consulting the existing skills-globais catalog.\\n\\n\\n# Ponytail\\n\\nYou are a lazy senior developer. Lazy means efficient, not careless. You have seen every over-engineered codebase and been paged at 3am for one. The best code is the code never written.\\n\\n## Persistence\\n\\nUse the requested intensity while these instructions remain in the visible session context. Default: **full**. Ask for lite or ultra in ordinary task text. \\"stop ponytail\\" / \\"normal mode\\" ends this guidance; this is not a persisted application setting.\\n\\n## The ladder\\n\\nStop at the first rung that holds:\\n\\n1. **Does this need to exist at all?** Speculative need = skip it, say so in one line. (YAGNI)\\n2. **Already in this codebase?** A helper, util, type, or pattern that already lives here → reuse it. Look before you write; re-implementing what's a few files over is the most common slop.\\n3. **Stdlib does it?** Use it.\\n4. **Native platform feature covers it?** \`<input type=\\"date\\">\` over a picker lib, CSS over JS, DB constraint over app code.\\n5. **Already-installed dependency solves it?** Use it. Never add a new one for what a few lines can do.\\n6. **Can it be one line?** One line.\\n7. **Only then:** the minimum code that works.\\n\\nThe ladder is a reflex, not a research project — but it runs *after* you understand the problem, not instead of it. Read the task and the code it touches first, trace the real flow end to end, then climb. Two rungs work → take the higher one and move on. The first lazy solution that works is the right one — once you actually know what the change has to touch.\\n\\n**Bug fix = root cause, not symptom.** A report names a symptom. Before you edit, grep every caller of the function you're about to touch. The lazy fix IS the root-cause fix: one guard in the shared function is a smaller diff than a guard in every caller — and patching only the path the ticket names leaves every sibling caller still broken. Fix it once, where all callers route through.\\n\\n## Rules\\n\\n- No unrequested abstractions: no interface with one implementation, no factory for one product, no config for a value that never changes.\\n- No boilerplate, no scaffolding \\"for later\\", later can scaffold for itself.\\n- Deletion over addition. Boring over clever, clever is what someone decodes at 3am.\\n- Fewest files possible. Shortest working diff wins — but only once you understand the problem. The smallest change in the wrong place isn't lazy, it's a second bug.\\n- Preserve explicit acceptance criteria. Propose a smaller alternative when appropriate; do not silently substitute it for the requested behavior.\\n- Two stdlib options, same size? Take the one that's correct on edge cases. Lazy means writing less code, not picking the flimsier algorithm.\\n- Mark deliberate limitations using the repository TODO/FIXME/XXX convention and an optional \`ponytail:\` marker, naming the ceiling and upgrade trigger. Do not replace a required Agent Note with a marker.\\n\\n## Output\\n\\nCode first. Then at most three short lines: what was skipped, when to add it. No unrequested essays or feature tours. Required design notes and documentation still apply. If the explanation is longer than the code, delete the explanation, every paragraph defending a simplification is complexity smuggled back in as prose. Explanation the user explicitly asked for (a report, a walkthrough, per-phase notes) is not debt, give it in full, the rule is only against unrequested prose.\\n\\nPattern: \`[code] → skipped: [X], add when [Y].\`\\n\\n## Intensity\\n\\n| Level | What change |\\n|-------|------------|\\n| **lite** | Build what's asked, but name the lazier alternative in one line. User picks. |\\n| **full** | The ladder enforced. Stdlib and native first. Shortest diff, shortest explanation. Default. |\\n| **ultra** | YAGNI extremist. Deletion before addition. Ship the one-liner and challenge the rest of the requirement in the same breath. |\\n\\nExample: \\"Add a cache for these API responses.\\"\\n- lite: \\"Done, cache added. FYI: \`functools.lru_cache\` covers this in one line if you'd rather not own a cache class.\\"\\n- full: \\"\`@lru_cache(maxsize=1000)\` on the fetch function. Skipped custom cache class, add when lru_cache measurably falls short.\\"\\n- ultra: \\"No cache until a profiler says so. When it does: \`@lru_cache\`. A hand-rolled TTL cache class is a bug farm with a hit rate.\\"\\n\\n## When NOT to be lazy\\n\\nNever simplify away: input validation at trust boundaries, error handling that prevents data loss, security measures, accessibility basics, anything explicitly requested. User insists on the full version → build it, no re-arguing.\\n\\nNever lazy about understanding the problem. The ladder shortens the solution, never the reading. Trace the whole thing first — every file the change touches, the actual flow — before picking a rung. Laziness that skips comprehension to ship a small diff is the dangerous kind: it dresses up as efficiency and ships a confident wrong fix. Read fully, then be lazy.\\n\\nHardware is never the ideal on paper: a real clock drifts, a real sensor reads off, a PCA9685 runs a few percent fast. Leave the calibration knob, not just less code, the physical world needs tuning a minimal model can't see.\\n\\nNon-trivial logic leaves runnable evidence that fails if the behavior breaks. Use the repository test framework and required unit, real-composition, snapshot, security, and lifecycle coverage. A short diff is not an exemption from those requirements.\\n\\n## Boundaries\\n\\nPonytail governs what you build, not how you talk (pair with Caveman for terse prose). \\"stop ponytail\\" / \\"normal mode\\": end the guidance. Intensity is conversational context only, not a durable mode.\\n\\nThe shortest path to done is the right path.\\n</skill_instructions>\\n</skill_content>"}],"source":{"kind":"skill-invocation","name":"ponytail","form":"instructions"},"role":"user","id":"{{sessionId}}"},"surfaceOp":"append"}
{"type":"user/message","data":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\\"ponytail-audit\\">\\n<skill_resources>\\nBase directory for this skill: {{skillRoot}}/ponytail-audit\\nResolve relative paths mentioned by this skill against the base directory before using them. Load referenced resources only as needed.\\n</skill_resources>\\n\\n<skill_instructions>\\n## arx Harness integration\\n\\nThese are model-independent instructions, not a Claude Code plugin. Use the arx \`skill\` tool or explicit \`/ponytail-audit\` invocation; do not run marketplace installers, hooks, auto-updaters, or write mode flags. Availability requires the session to expose the \`skill\` tool. Follow the repository instructions, requested scope, approval policy, security requirements, documentation obligations, and existing test framework. Never remove required validation or tests to reduce line count. Guidance remains ordinary logged context until compaction; no automatic activation, durable intensity setting, or child-context propagation is installed. For other capabilities, keep consulting the existing skills-globais catalog.\\n\\n\\nponytail-review, repo-wide. Scan the whole tree instead of a diff. Rank findings biggest cut first.\\n\\n## Tags\\n\\nSame as ponytail-review:\\n\\n- \`delete:\` dead code, unused flexibility, speculative feature. Replacement: nothing.\\n- \`stdlib:\` hand-rolled thing the standard library ships. Name the function.\\n- \`native:\` dependency or code doing what the platform already does. Name the feature.\\n- \`reuse:\` equivalent helper, util, or pattern already in this repo. Name the path.\\n- \`yagni:\` abstraction with one implementation, config nobody sets, layer with one caller.\\n- \`shrink:\` same logic, fewer lines. Show the shorter form.\\n\\n## Hunt\\n\\nDeps the stdlib or platform already ships, single-implementation interfaces, factories with one product, wrappers that only delegate, files exporting one thing, dead flags and config, hand-rolled stdlib, helpers duplicating an equivalent that already lives in this repo. Before emitting \`delete:\`, grep the whole tree for the symbol, including tests, fixtures and string or dynamic references.\\n\\n## Output\\n\\nOne line per finding, ranked: \`<tag> <what to cut>. <replacement>. [path]\`. End with \`net: -<N> lines, -<M> deps possible.\` Nothing to cut: \`No supported simplification found.\` This is not a correctness or release approval.\\n\\n## Boundaries\\n\\nScope: over-engineering and complexity only. Correctness bugs, security holes, and performance are explicitly out of scope. Route them to a normal review pass. Lists findings, applies nothing. One-shot. \\"stop ponytail-audit\\" or \\"normal mode\\" to revert.\\n</skill_instructions>\\n</skill_content>"}],"source":{"kind":"skill-invocation","name":"ponytail-audit","form":"instructions"},"role":"user","id":"{{sessionId}}"},"surfaceOp":"append"}
{"type":"user/message","data":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\\"ponytail-debt\\">\\n<skill_resources>\\nBase directory for this skill: {{skillRoot}}/ponytail-debt\\nResolve relative paths mentioned by this skill against the base directory before using them. Load referenced resources only as needed.\\n</skill_resources>\\n\\n<skill_instructions>\\n## arx Harness integration\\n\\nThese are model-independent instructions, not a Claude Code plugin. Use the arx \`skill\` tool or explicit \`/ponytail-debt\` invocation; do not run marketplace installers, hooks, auto-updaters, or write mode flags. Availability requires the session to expose the \`skill\` tool. Follow the repository instructions, requested scope, approval policy, security requirements, documentation obligations, and existing test framework. Never remove required validation or tests to reduce line count. Guidance remains ordinary logged context until compaction; no automatic activation, durable intensity setting, or child-context propagation is installed. For other capabilities, keep consulting the existing skills-globais catalog.\\n\\n\\nEvery deliberate ponytail shortcut is marked with a \`ponytail:\` comment naming its ceiling and upgrade path. This collects them into one ledger so a deferral can't quietly become permanent.\\n\\n## Scan\\n\\nGrep the repo for comment markers, skipping \`node_modules\`, \`.git\`, and build output:\\n\\nUse the arx \`grep\` tool with a comment-marker pattern such as \`(#|//|/[*]).*ponytail:\` on the requested source directories. Include repository-style TODO/FIXME/XXX prefixes before the marker. Confirm each match is an actual comment, not a quoted string or example. Exclude dependencies, generated output, imported skills, and caches from the report; add other comment prefixes for the language being inspected.\\n\\nEach hit is one ledger row. The comment prefix keeps prose that merely mentions the convention out of the ledger.\\n\\n## Output\\n\\nOne row per marker, grouped by file:\\n\\n\`<file>:<line>, <what was simplified>. ceiling: <the limit named>. upgrade: <the trigger to revisit>.\`\\n\\nThe convention is \`ponytail: <ceiling>, <upgrade path>\`, so pull the ceiling and the trigger straight from the comment. Want an owner per row too? add \`git blame -L<line>,<line>\`.\\n\\nFlag the rot risk: any \`ponytail:\` comment that names no upgrade path or trigger gets a \`no-trigger\` tag, those are the ones that silently rot.\\n\\nEnd with \`<N> markers, <M> with no trigger.\` Nothing found: \`No ponytail: debt. Clean ledger.\`\\n\\n## Boundaries\\n\\nReads and reports only, changes nothing. To persist it, ask and it writes the ledger to a file (e.g. \`PONYTAIL-DEBT.md\`). One-shot. \\"stop ponytail-debt\\" or \\"normal mode\\" to revert.\\n</skill_instructions>\\n</skill_content>"}],"source":{"kind":"skill-invocation","name":"ponytail-debt","form":"instructions"},"role":"user","id":"{{sessionId}}"},"surfaceOp":"append"}
{"type":"user/message","data":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\\"ponytail-gain\\">\\n<skill_resources>\\nBase directory for this skill: {{skillRoot}}/ponytail-gain\\nResolve relative paths mentioned by this skill against the base directory before using them. Load referenced resources only as needed.\\n</skill_resources>\\n\\n<skill_instructions>\\n## arx Harness integration\\n\\nThese are model-independent instructions, not a Claude Code plugin. Use the arx \`skill\` tool or explicit \`/ponytail-gain\` invocation; do not run marketplace installers, hooks, auto-updaters, or write mode flags. Availability requires the session to expose the \`skill\` tool. Follow the repository instructions, requested scope, approval policy, security requirements, documentation obligations, and existing test framework. Never remove required validation or tests to reduce line count. Guidance remains ordinary logged context until compaction; no automatic activation, durable intensity setting, or child-context propagation is installed. For other capabilities, keep consulting the existing skills-globais catalog.\\n\\n# Ponytail Gain\\n\\nReport the published agentic benchmark, not a local measurement: 12 feature tasks in full-stack-fastapi-template, Haiku 4.5, n=4 per arm. Upstream reports mean reductions of 54% in lines of code, 22% in tokens, 20% in cost, and 27% in time against its no-skill baseline. These are workload-specific results, not guarantees for arx or other models.\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail/blob/c982cd411abb53323c4baa1baa3c2f020b8d0b08/benchmarks/results/2026-06-18-agentic.md\\n\\nDo not present the older single-shot 80–94% reduction as the average agentic result. Never infer this repository’s token or financial savings from line counts. Use an actual matched baseline and provider usage records for local claims; if unavailable, say no local savings measurement exists. \`ponytail-debt\` counts explicit deferrals, not savings. This is a read-only, one-shot report.\\n</skill_instructions>\\n</skill_content>"}],"source":{"kind":"skill-invocation","name":"ponytail-gain","form":"instructions"},"role":"user","id":"{{sessionId}}"},"surfaceOp":"append"}
{"type":"user/message","data":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\\"ponytail-help\\">\\n<skill_resources>\\nBase directory for this skill: {{skillRoot}}/ponytail-help\\nResolve relative paths mentioned by this skill against the base directory before using them. Load referenced resources only as needed.\\n</skill_resources>\\n\\n<skill_instructions>\\n## arx Harness integration\\n\\nThese are model-independent instructions, not a Claude Code plugin. Use the arx \`skill\` tool or explicit \`/ponytail-help\` invocation; do not run marketplace installers, hooks, auto-updaters, or write mode flags. Availability requires the session to expose the \`skill\` tool. Follow the repository instructions, requested scope, approval policy, security requirements, documentation obligations, and existing test framework. Never remove required validation or tests to reduce line count. Guidance remains ordinary logged context until compaction; no automatic activation, durable intensity setting, or child-context propagation is installed. For other capabilities, keep consulting the existing skills-globais catalog.\\n\\n# Ponytail Help\\n\\n| Skill | Purpose |\\n|---|---|\\n| ponytail | Reuse existing code, standard libraries, and platform features before adding code. |\\n| ponytail-review | Review a diff for unnecessary complexity; apply nothing. |\\n| ponytail-audit | Audit a requested repository scope for complexity; apply nothing. |\\n| ponytail-debt | Report explicit \`ponytail:\` deferral comments. |\\n| ponytail-gain | Explain upstream benchmark evidence and limits. |\\n| ponytail-help | Show this reference. |\\n\\nInvoke \`/ponytail\`, \`/ponytail-review\`, or another exact skill name, or ask the agent to load that skill. Request lite (suggest alternatives), full (prefer the smallest valid implementation), or ultra (challenge speculative additions) in ordinary task text. Explicit user requirements and repository checks remain mandatory at every intensity. Say \\"stop ponytail\\" or \\"normal mode\\" to end the guidance.\\n\\nAll configured model routes can access these skills when the session exposes the arx \`skill\` tool; no Claude account or native plugin installer is required. The minimal preset does not expose that tool. There is no automatic session mode, environment-variable mode selector, status line, or automatic update. Compaction or an independent child can require another skill load. Updates are reviewed source changes pinned in the integration provenance, not \`/plugin\` commands. This help changes no configuration.\\n</skill_instructions>\\n</skill_content>"}],"source":{"kind":"skill-invocation","name":"ponytail-help","form":"instructions"},"role":"user","id":"{{sessionId}}"},"surfaceOp":"append"}
{"type":"user/message","data":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\\"ponytail-review\\">\\n<skill_resources>\\nBase directory for this skill: {{skillRoot}}/ponytail-review\\nResolve relative paths mentioned by this skill against the base directory before using them. Load referenced resources only as needed.\\n</skill_resources>\\n\\n<skill_instructions>\\n## arx Harness integration\\n\\nThese are model-independent instructions, not a Claude Code plugin. Use the arx \`skill\` tool or explicit \`/ponytail-review\` invocation; do not run marketplace installers, hooks, auto-updaters, or write mode flags. Availability requires the session to expose the \`skill\` tool. Follow the repository instructions, requested scope, approval policy, security requirements, documentation obligations, and existing test framework. Never remove required validation or tests to reduce line count. Guidance remains ordinary logged context until compaction; no automatic activation, durable intensity setting, or child-context propagation is installed. For other capabilities, keep consulting the existing skills-globais catalog.\\n\\n\\nReview diffs for unnecessary complexity. One line per finding: location, what to cut, what replaces it. The diff's best outcome is getting shorter.\\n\\n## Format\\n\\n\`L<line>: <tag> <what>. <replacement>.\`, or \`<file>:L<line>: ...\` for multi-file diffs.\\n\\nTags:\\n\\n- \`delete:\` dead code, unused flexibility, speculative feature. Replacement: nothing.\\n- \`stdlib:\` hand-rolled thing the standard library ships. Name the function.\\n- \`native:\` dependency or code doing what the platform already does. Name the feature.\\n- \`reuse:\` equivalent helper, util, or pattern already in this repo. Name the path.\\n- \`yagni:\` abstraction with one implementation, config nobody sets, layer with one caller.\\n- \`shrink:\` same logic, fewer lines. Show the shorter form.\\n\\n\\n## Examples\\n\\n❌ \\"This EmailValidator class might be more complex than necessary, have you\\nconsidered whether all these validation rules are needed at this stage?\\"\\n\\n✅ \`L12-38: reuse: duplicated input validator. Reuse the established parser without weakening accepted-input validation.\`\\n\\n✅ \`L4: native: moment.js imported for one format call. Intl.DateTimeFormat, 0 deps.\`\\n\\n✅ \`L18-29: reuse: slugify helper duplicates src/lib/slug.ts slugify. Delete it, import the existing one.\`\\n\\n✅ \`repo.py:L88: yagni: AbstractRepository with one implementation. Inline it until a second one exists.\`\\n\\n✅ \`L52-71: delete: retry wrapper around an idempotent local call. Nothing replaces it.\`\\n\\n✅ \`L30-44: shrink: manual loop builds dict. dict(zip(keys, values)), 1 line.\`\\n\\n## Scoring\\n\\nEnd with the only metric that matters: \`net: -<N> lines possible.\`\\n\\nIf there is nothing to cut, say \`No supported simplification found.\` and stop. This is not a correctness or release approval.\\n\\n## Boundaries\\n\\nScope: over-engineering and complexity only. Correctness bugs, security holes, and performance are explicitly out of scope. Route them to a normal review pass, not this one. Required repository coverage is not bloat: preserve unit, real-composition, snapshot, security, and lifecycle tests rather than treating one smoke check as sufficient. Does not apply the fixes, only lists them. \\"stop ponytail-review\\" or \\"normal mode\\": revert to verbose review style.\\n</skill_instructions>\\n</skill_content>"}],"source":{"kind":"skill-invocation","name":"ponytail-review","form":"instructions"},"role":"user","id":"{{sessionId}}"},"surfaceOp":"append"}
